          "_name": "article08",
          "_sourceTheme": "startm5",
          "_customHTML": "<section class=\"zl-page\">\n<div class=\"zl-wrap\">\n<nav class=\"zl-crumbs\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><ol><li><a href=\"index.html\">Home</a></li><li><a href=\"guides.html\">Guides</a></li><li aria-current=\"page\">Health Insurance and Seeing a Doctor in Germany</li></ol></nav>\n<header>\n<span class=\"zl-tag\">Guides</span>\n<h1>Health Insurance and Seeing a Doctor in Germany</h1>\n<p class=\"zl-lead\">Once you are registered with the Jobcenter or start a job, you are covered by statutory health insurance. Knowing which door to knock on, family doctor, on-call service or emergency room, saves hours of waiting.</p>\n<p class=\"zl-meta\"><span>Updated: <time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">26 October 2026</time></span><span>5 min read</span><span>ZusammenLeben</span></p>\n</header>\n<article class=\"zl-card zl-body\"><h2>How you get insured</h2>\n<p>Health insurance is compulsory in Germany. People from Ukraine who receive benefits from the Jobcenter are insured through it in a statutory health insurance fund (<em>gesetzliche Krankenkasse</em>), for example AOK, TK, Barmer or DAK. You choose the fund yourself and tell the Jobcenter. If you work in a regular job, you are insured through your employer, and children and a non-working spouse are usually covered at no extra cost through family insurance (<em>Familienversicherung</em>). After registration you receive an electronic health card (<em>elektronische Gesundheitskarte</em>) with your photo. Bring it to every appointment, also for your children.</p>\n<p>Until the card arrives, the fund can issue a temporary confirmation of insurance. Keep it with your documents. If you change address or name, inform the fund.</p>\n<h2>What statutory insurance covers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Visits to family doctors and specialists, and hospital treatment</li>\n<li>Prescription medicines, with a small co-payment for adults</li>\n<li>Pregnancy care, birth and check-ups for children (<em>U-Untersuchungen</em>)</li>\n<li>Vaccinations recommended in Germany</li>\n<li>Basic dental treatment; dentures and some treatments only partly</li>\n<li>Psychotherapy after an initial consultation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Glasses for adults, many cosmetic treatments and some extra services are not covered or only in part. Ask before treatment if you are unsure, and never sign a private cost agreement without understanding it.</p>\n<h2>Start with a family doctor (Hausarzt)</h2>\n<p>In Germany, the family doctor is the first point of contact for most health problems. He or she knows your history, prescribes medicines and refers you to specialists (<em>Überweisung</em>) when needed. Choose a practice near your home and stay with it. For children, a paediatrician (<em>Kinderarzt</em>) takes this role and carries out the regular check-ups, which are noted in the yellow check-up booklet.</p>\n<p>Many practices in Hof and the surrounding towns are full. Call several, ask whether they accept new patients (<em>Neupatienten</em>), and say if it is urgent. The patient service 116 117 can also help you find a practice and, for some referrals, book an appointment with a specialist.</p>\n<h2>Which number for which situation</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>112</strong>: life-threatening emergency, ambulance and fire brigade. Free, 24 hours, from any phone.</li>\n<li><strong>116 117</strong>: on-call medical service when your doctor's practice is closed, at night, weekends and holidays. A doctor can advise by phone or visit you at home.</li>\n<li><strong>Pharmacy emergency service</strong>: at night one pharmacy in each area stays open. The address is posted on every pharmacy door and online.</li>\n<li><strong>Poison emergency</strong>: the poison information centre for Bavaria answers around the clock; the number is listed on our <a href=\"emergency.html\">emergency page</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"zl-note zl-note--warn\"><strong>Note:</strong> go to the hospital emergency room (<em>Notaufnahme</em>) only for real emergencies, such as chest pain, breathing problems, serious injuries or signs of a stroke. For a fever or a cold, 116 117 is the right number and the wait is much shorter.</div>\n<h2>At the practice</h2>\n<p>Bring your health card, a list of medicines you take and any medical documents from Ukraine, even untranslated; doctors often understand diagnoses and test results. Arrive a few minutes early to fill in forms. Appointments with specialists can take weeks; if you have an urgent referral, the practice can mark it. Doctors in Germany are bound by professional confidentiality, also towards authorities.</p>\n<h2>Interpreting and language</h2>\n<p>Ask whether the practice has staff who speak Ukrainian or Russian. Some towns keep lists of doctors by language. If needed, bring someone who can translate, or use a translation app for simple questions. For important conversations, for example before surgery, insist on a proper interpreter; ask the hospital's social service how to arrange one.</p>\n<h2>Prescriptions and pharmacies</h2>\n<p>Prescription medicines (<em>verschreibungspflichtig</em>) cost adults a co-payment of a few euros per package at the pharmacy (<em>Apotheke</em>); children are exempt. Many prescriptions are now electronic and are read from your health card. Some simple medicines are sold without prescription and are paid in full. If co-payments become a burden, you can apply to your fund for an exemption once you have reached the yearly limit, which depends on your income; keep all receipts.</p>\n<h2>Dentists</h2>\n<p>Statutory insurance covers check-ups, fillings, root canal treatment and tooth extraction. An annual check-up is worth it: it is free, and a stamp in the bonus booklet (<em>Bonusheft</em>) increases the share the fund pays for dentures later. For dentures, crowns and orthodontics, the practice prepares a treatment and cost plan (<em>Heil- und Kostenplan</em>) that the fund must approve first. With a low income, the fund may cover a larger share; ask about the hardship rule (<em>Härtefallregelung</em>). Children and teenagers receive free preventive care, including sealing of molars.</p>\n<h2>A hospital stay</h2>\n<p>For planned treatment you need a referral for hospital (<em>Krankenhauseinweisung</em>) from your doctor. Bring your health card, ID, medicines and documents. Adults pay a small daily co-payment for a limited number of days per year. Every hospital has a social service (<em>Sozialdienst</em>) that helps with interpreting, rehabilitation and care after discharge. After a stay, you receive a discharge letter (<em>Entlassbrief</em>); give it to your family doctor.</p>\n<p>Medical aids such as wheelchairs, hearing aids or orthopaedic insoles are prescribed by a doctor and covered by the fund, sometimes with a small co-payment. The fund must approve them before you order, so let the supplier submit the prescription first.</p>\n<h2>Vaccinations and documents from Ukraine</h2>\n<p>Bring any vaccination records you have, even if incomplete. If records are missing, your doctor can check immunity or plan catch-up vaccinations according to the German recommendations. Translations of diagnoses are helpful for chronic illnesses; your doctor will tell you which documents matter.</p>\n<h2>Mental health</h2>\n<p>Stress, sleep problems and anxiety are common after fleeing war. Your family doctor is a good first contact. You can also book an initial consultation directly with a psychotherapist through 116 117. Free counselling centres, crisis lines and services in Ukrainian exist; see our <a href=\"emergency.html\">emergency page</a> for numbers. Asking for help is normal in Germany and is covered by insurance.</p>\n<h2>Pregnancy and children's health</h2>\n<p>Pregnant women are entitled to regular check-ups with a gynaecologist, a maternity record (<em>Mutterpass</em>) and support from a midwife (<em>Hebamme</em>) before and after birth, all covered by insurance. Start looking for a midwife early, as they are in high demand. For children, the check-ups U1 to U9 and the vaccination schedule are important; schools and Kitas ask for proof of measles vaccination.</p>\n<h2>Useful official sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.germany4ukraine.de/EN/medizinische-versorgung/seite_node.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Germany4Ukraine: medical care</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.116117.de/en/index.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">116117: on-call medical service (English)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.bundesgesundheitsministerium.de/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Federal Ministry of Health</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tk.de/en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Techniker Krankenkasse: information in English</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"zl-disclaimer\">General information, not legal advice. Rules change; always check the official sources linked above.</p>\n</article>\n<div class=\"zl-faq zl-body\"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<details><summary>Who pays my health insurance?</summary><p>If you receive Jobcenter benefits, the Jobcenter pays the contributions. If you work, you are insured through your employer.</p></details>\n<details><summary>What number do I call when the doctor is closed?</summary><p>Call 116 117 for the on-call medical service. In a life-threatening emergency call 112.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Do I need a family doctor?</summary><p>It is strongly recommended. The family doctor coordinates your care and refers you to specialists.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Are my children insured too?</summary><p>Yes. Children are insured through the Jobcenter or through family insurance at no extra cost.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Is psychotherapy covered?</summary><p>Yes. After an initial consultation, psychotherapy is covered by statutory health insurance.</p></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"zl-cta\"><h2>Need personal help?</h2><p>Our volunteers answer in Ukrainian, Russian, German and English. Free of charge.</p><div class=\"zl-btns\"><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--wa\" href=\"https://wa.me/380637004615\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Message us on WhatsApp</a><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--light\" href=\"Contact.html\">Contact form</a></div></div>\n<div class=\"zl-related\"><h2>More guides</h2><div class=\"zl-grid\">\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-recognition-qualifications.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"4\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" rx=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"17\" r=\"3\"/><path d=\"M10 20l-1 2M14 20l1 2M7 8h10M7 11h6\"/></svg></span><h3>Getting Your Ukrainian Qualifications Recognised in Germany</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-integration-course.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M4 5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h13v16H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2z\"/><path d=\"M4 19V5\"/><path d=\"M8 7h7\"/></svg></span><h3>The Integration Course: Your Fastest Route to German</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-residence-permit-24.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"5\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" rx=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"9\" cy=\"11\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"M6 16c1-2 5-2 6 0M14 10h4M14 13h3\"/></svg></span><h3>Your §24 Residence Permit: What You Need to Know</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n</div><p style=\"margin-top:14px\"><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--ghost\" href=\"guides.html\">All guides</a></p></div>\n</div>\n</section>",
