          "_name": "article08",
          "_sourceTheme": "startm5",
          "_customHTML": "<section class=\"zl-page\">\n<div class=\"zl-wrap\">\n<nav class=\"zl-crumbs\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><ol><li><a href=\"index.html\">Home</a></li><li><a href=\"guides.html\">Guides</a></li><li aria-current=\"page\">Bank Account, Phone Contract and Broadcasting Fee</li></ol></nav>\n<header>\n<span class=\"zl-tag\">Guides</span>\n<h1>Bank Account, Phone Contract and Broadcasting Fee</h1>\n<p class=\"zl-lead\">A German bank account is needed for benefits, wages and rent. A phone number is needed for every authority. And sooner or later a letter about the broadcasting fee arrives. Here is how to handle all three.</p>\n<p class=\"zl-meta\"><span>Updated: <time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">26 October 2026</time></span><span>5 min read</span><span>ZusammenLeben</span></p>\n</header>\n<article class=\"zl-card zl-body\"><h2>Three things every household needs</h2>\n<p>A German bank account is needed for benefits, wages and rent. A phone number is needed for every authority, school and doctor. And sooner or later a letter about the broadcasting fee arrives. None of this is complicated once you know the rules, but mistakes here can lead to fees, contracts you did not want, or debts. This guide explains the basics.</p>\n<h2>Your right to a basic bank account</h2>\n<p>Everyone who lives legally in the EU has the right to a basic payment account (<em>Basiskonto</em>), including people with temporary protection. A bank may not refuse it just because you have no German credit history or a low income. Bring your passport or residence permit and your registration certificate. If a bank refuses, ask for the refusal in writing; you can complain to the financial supervisory authority (BaFin).</p>\n<p>Local savings banks (<em>Sparkasse</em>), cooperative banks (<em>Volksbank</em>, <em>Raiffeisenbank</em>) and online banks all offer accounts. Compare the monthly fee, the cost of withdrawing cash and whether you get a debit card (<em>Girocard</em>). A local branch is useful if you need help in person; online banks are often cheaper.</p>\n<h2>How payments work in Germany</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bank transfer</strong> (<em>Überweisung</em>): you send money with the recipient's name, IBAN and a reference. Rent is usually paid this way, often as a standing order (<em>Dauerauftrag</em>) on a fixed date.</li>\n<li><strong>Direct debit</strong> (<em>Lastschrift</em>): you allow a company, for example the phone provider or electricity supplier, to collect money. You can reverse an unjustified direct debit within eight weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Cash and cards:</strong> cash is still common, especially in small shops and bakeries. Debit cards are accepted in most supermarkets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Check your account statements every month. Report changes in your account details to the Jobcenter, employer and landlord in good time, so that payments do not go to an old account.</p>\n<h2>Online banking safely</h2>\n<p>Most banks offer an app for online banking with a second confirmation step, for example a push message or a code generator. Install the app only from the official app store, use a strong password and never share confirmation codes. If you lose your card or notice a strange payment, block the card immediately through the central blocking number 116 116, which works for most German bank cards.</p>\n<h2>Phone and internet</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>prepaid SIM</strong> has no contract and no risk. You top up credit when you need it. It is the best start.</li>\n<li>A <strong>contract</strong> usually runs 24 months. Read the minimum term, the notice period and the price after the first months before signing. Since 2022, contracts can be cancelled monthly after the minimum term.</li>\n<li>A <strong>home internet connection</strong> (DSL, cable or fibre) also has a minimum term. Check availability at your address first, and ask your landlord whether a connection already exists.</li>\n<li>For SIM cards, you need to identify yourself with a passport or residence permit. Never give your documents to others to register a SIM for them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Protect yourself from scams and doorstep sales</h2>\n<p>Be careful with door-to-door sellers and phone calls offering electricity, phone or insurance contracts. You can usually cancel a contract signed at the door or by phone within 14 days (<em>Widerruf</em>); do it in writing and keep proof. Never give your bank details, PIN or online banking codes over the phone. Banks and authorities never ask for them. Messages that ask you to \"confirm\" your account, pay a parcel fee or claim a prize are almost always fraud.</p>\n<h2>The broadcasting fee (Rundfunkbeitrag)</h2>\n<p>Every household in Germany pays one broadcasting fee, regardless of how many people live there or whether you own a TV or radio. It funds public broadcasters. After you register your address, you will receive a letter from the \"ARD ZDF Deutschlandradio Beitragsservice\". Do not ignore it: unpaid fees grow with reminders and can end up with enforcement.</p>\n<p>If you receive benefits from the Jobcenter or social welfare office, you can apply for an exemption (<em>Befreiung</em>) with a copy of your benefit decision. The exemption applies for the period of the decision. If several adults live in one apartment, only one person needs to pay or be exempted.</p>\n<div class=\"zl-note\"><strong>Tip:</strong> apply for the exemption immediately and again each time you receive a new benefit decision. The exemption is not automatic, and it is usually not granted for past months.</div>\n<h2>Electricity and energy bills</h2>\n<p>In most apartments you sign your own electricity contract with a supplier of your choice. You pay a fixed monthly instalment (<em>Abschlag</em>), and once a year the supplier reads the meter and sends a statement: if you used more, you pay the difference; if less, you get money back. Write down the meter reading when you move in and out. Compare suppliers on independent comparison portals, but avoid offers that demand payment in advance. If you receive benefits, send large back payments to the Jobcenter; heating costs are usually covered, household electricity is part of the standard amount.</p>\n<h2>Child benefit</h2>\n<p>Parents with a §24 permit can receive child benefit (<em>Kindergeld</em>) for each child. You apply to the family benefits office (<em>Familienkasse</em>) of the employment agency, with the tax IDs of parent and child. It is paid monthly to your account. If you receive Jobcenter benefits, child benefit counts as the child's income and reduces the benefits accordingly, but you still have to apply for it.</p>\n<h2>Insurance worth knowing</h2>\n<p>Besides health insurance, one private insurance is strongly recommended for every household: personal liability insurance (<em>Privathaftpflichtversicherung</em>). It pays if you accidentally damage something belonging to someone else, for example a neighbour's property or the landlord's flooring. It is inexpensive and many landlords expect it. Be cautious with other insurance offers; many are unnecessary. Independent consumer advice centres can check offers for you.</p>\n<h2>Protect yourself from debt</h2>\n<p>Never sign contracts you do not understand, and open every letter. If you cannot pay a bill, contact the company early and ask for instalments. If debts appear, free debt counselling (<em>Schuldnerberatung</em>) from welfare organisations helps you sort things out and negotiate with creditors. You can also convert your account into a protected account (<em>Pfändungsschutzkonto</em>, P-Konto), which protects a basic amount of money from seizure. Ask us for contacts in your town.</p>\n<h2>How we help</h2>\n<p>We help with opening accounts, reading contracts, applying for the broadcasting fee exemption and finding debt counselling. <a href=\"request-assistance.html\">Ask for help</a>.</p>\n<h2>Useful official sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.germany4ukraine.de/EN/online-services/seite_node.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Germany4Ukraine: online services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rundfunkbeitrag.de/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rundfunkbeitrag: official broadcasting fee website</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.verbraucherzentrale.de/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Consumer advice centre (Verbraucherzentrale)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://handbookgermany.de/en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Handbook Germany: money and everyday life</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"zl-disclaimer\">General information, not legal advice. Rules change; always check the official sources linked above.</p>\n</article>\n<div class=\"zl-faq zl-body\"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<details><summary>Can a bank refuse me an account?</summary><p>Everyone living legally in the EU has the right to a basic account (Basiskonto). A bank may only refuse it for narrow legal reasons.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Do I have to pay the broadcasting fee?</summary><p>Every household pays it, but people who receive Jobcenter benefits can apply for an exemption.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Can I cancel a contract signed at the door?</summary><p>In most cases yes, within 14 days. Cancel in writing and keep proof.</p></details>\n<details><summary>What is a P-Konto?</summary><p>A protected account that keeps a basic amount of money safe from seizure if you have debts. Any account can be converted.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Do I need liability insurance?</summary><p>It is not compulsory, but strongly recommended. It is inexpensive and covers damage you cause to others.</p></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"zl-cta\"><h2>Need personal help?</h2><p>Our volunteers answer in Ukrainian, Russian, German and English. Free of charge.</p><div class=\"zl-btns\"><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--wa\" href=\"https://wa.me/380637004615\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Message us on WhatsApp</a><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--light\" href=\"Contact.html\">Contact form</a></div></div>\n<div class=\"zl-related\"><h2>More guides</h2><div class=\"zl-grid\">\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-driving-licence.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M5 16l1.5-5h11L19 16\"/><rect x=\"3\" y=\"16\" width=\"18\" height=\"4\" rx=\"1\"/><circle cx=\"7.5\" cy=\"18\" r=\"1\"/><circle cx=\"16.5\" cy=\"18\" r=\"1\"/></svg></span><h3>Driving in Germany with a Ukrainian Licence</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-finding-apartment.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M3 11l9-7 9 7\"/><path d=\"M5 10v10h14V10\"/><path d=\"M10 20v-6h4v6\"/></svg></span><h3>Finding an Apartment in Upper Franconia with Jobcenter Support</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-jobcenter-letters.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"5\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M3 7l9 6 9-6\"/></svg></span><h3>How to Read and Answer Jobcenter Letters</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n</div><p style=\"margin-top:14px\"><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--ghost\" href=\"guides.html\">All guides</a></p></div>\n</div>\n</section>",
